    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for any confusion caused by my previous response. In Singapore, the courts have established that a general force majeure clause in a lease agreement can be interpreted to cover unforeseen events, including human-driven disruptions, such as civil unrest, that are not explicitly listed in the agreement.\n\nHowever, the courts do require that the event must be reasonably foreseeable to be considered a force majeure event. This means that the parties must have taken reasonable precautions to mitigate the risk of the disruption.\n\nRegarding the specific type of event, the courts have held that a general force majeure clause can be applied to cover unforeseen events, even if they are not explicitly listed in the contract. This is because the courts consider the purpose of a force majeure clause to be to provide relief in situations where the parties cannot reasonably be expected to perform their obligations due to circumstances beyond their control.\n\nIn the case of civil unrest, the courts may consider whether the parties had reason to anticipate the disruption and take steps to mitigate its impact. If the parties had taken reasonable precautions, the court may not consider the civil unrest to be a force majeure event, even if it was not explicitly listed in the lease agreement.\n\nIt's worth noting that the Singapore",
